/-!
# The exponential map of a complex elliptic curve

The points `E(ℂ)` of an elliptic curve over `ℂ` form a compact commutative complex Lie group
(`WeierstrassCurve.Projective.Point.instLieAddGroup`), built from the projective Weierstrass
equation alone. This file constructs its **exponential map** `exp_E : ℂ → E(ℂ)` from the flows of
invariant vector fields. The definition of `exp_E`, its holomorphy, its openness and the fact that
its kernel is a full lattice do not use the Weierstrass `℘`-function. The one further global input
is the connectedness of `E(ℂ)` (`WeierstrassCurve.Projective.Point.instConnectedSpace`), which is
currently proved through the `℘`-uniformization of `E(ℂ)`; it is used for surjectivity, so
`WeierstrassCurve.Projective.exp_surjective`, `WeierstrassCurve.Projective.isCoveringMap_exp` and
`WeierstrassCurve.Projective.expQuotientKerContinuousAddEquiv` depend on `℘` through it.

Regard `E(ℂ)` as a real Lie group by restricting scalars. Its tangent space at the origin is `ℂ`,
and a tangent vector `v` generates a translation-invariant vector field. This field is complete, and
its flow for time one carries the origin to `exp_E v`. As `E(ℂ)` is commutative, `exp_E` is a
group homomorphism, so `exp_E (x + i y)` is the sum in `E(ℂ)` of the points reached from the
origin at time `x` along the invariant integral curve generated by `1` and at time `y` along the
one generated by `i` (`WeierstrassCurve.Projective.exp_eq_mulInvariantIntegralCurve_re_add_im`).

The tangent space at the origin is identified with `ℂ` through the chart of `E(ℂ)` at the origin
(`WeierstrassCurve.Projective.Point.instChartedSpace`), as for every manifold modelled on `ℂ`. That
chart is not normalized against the invariant differential `dx / (2y + a₁x + a₃)`: another chart
at the origin would replace `exp_E` by a rescaling `z ↦ exp_E (c z)` with `c ∈ ℂˣ`. Every
statement in this file is invariant under such a rescaling. Normalizing `exp_E` so that the
invariant differential pulls back to `dz`, as is needed to identify its kernel with
`E.periodLattice`, is part of its comparison with the invariant differential, which this file does
not attempt.

The general theory of the exponential map of a commutative real Lie group then gives the global
structure of `exp_E`. It is an open map, and it is onto because `E(ℂ)` is connected. Its kernel is
discrete. Since `E(ℂ)` is compact, the kernel spans `ℂ` over `ℝ`, so it is a full lattice, and it
is the lattice of a period pair. Hence `exp_E` is a covering map and induces an isomorphism of
topological groups `ℂ / ker exp_E ≃ E(ℂ)`.

Finally, `exp_E` is holomorphic. Read in the chart of `E(ℂ)` at the origin, its real derivative at
`0` is the identity, which is complex linear, so `exp_E` is complex differentiable at `0`. Near
any other point `z` it is `w ↦ exp_E z + exp_E (w - z)`, a translate of its germ at `0`, and
translations of `E(ℂ)` are holomorphic (`TauCeti.contMDiff_mulInvariantExp_complex`, for every
commutative complex Lie group). So `exp_E` is an entire group homomorphism.

## Main definitions

* `WeierstrassCurve.Projective.exp`: the exponential map `exp_E : ℂ →+ E(ℂ)`.
* `WeierstrassCurve.Projective.expQuotientKerContinuousAddEquiv`: the induced isomorphism of
  topological groups `ℂ / ker exp_E ≃ₜ+ E(ℂ)`.

## Main results

* `WeierstrassCurve.Projective.exp_eq_mulInvariantExp`: `exp_E` is the tangent-space exponential of
  the underlying real Lie group.
* `WeierstrassCurve.Projective.exp_eq_mulInvariantIntegralCurve_re_add_im`: `exp_E (x + i y)` is
  the sum of the invariant integral curves through the origin generated by `1` at time `x` and by
  `i` at time `y`.
* `WeierstrassCurve.Projective.exp_surjective`, `WeierstrassCurve.Projective.isOpenMap_exp` and
  `WeierstrassCurve.Projective.isCoveringMap_exp`: `exp_E` is an open covering map onto `E(ℂ)`.
* `WeierstrassCurve.Projective.span_ker_exp_eq_top` and the instances
  `DiscreteTopology W.exp.ker` and `IsZLattice ℝ W.exp.ker.toIntSubmodule`: the kernel of `exp_E`
  is a full lattice.
* `WeierstrassCurve.Projective.exists_periodPair_lattice_eq_ker_exp`: the kernel of `exp_E` is the
  lattice of a period pair.
* `WeierstrassCurve.Projective.hasFDerivAt_extChartAt_exp_zero`: in the chart at the origin,
  `exp_E` has complex derivative the identity at `0`.
* `WeierstrassCurve.Projective.mdifferentiable_exp` and
  `WeierstrassCurve.Projective.contMDiff_exp`: `exp_E` is holomorphic.

## References

* J. H. Silverman, *The Arithmetic of Elliptic Curves*, 2nd ed., Graduate Texts in Mathematics
  106, Springer, 2009, §VI.5.
* J. M. Lee, *Introduction to Smooth Manifolds*, 2nd ed., Graduate Texts in Mathematics 218,
  Springer, 2013, Chapter 20.
-/

public section

open Complex
open scoped ContDiff Manifold

namespace WeierstrassCurve.Projective

variable (W : Projective ℂ) [W.IsElliptic]

/-! ### The underlying real Lie group

The Lie-group exponential is developed for multiplicative real Lie groups, so it is applied to the
type tag `Multiplicative W.Point`, which keeps the charts and the smooth structure of `W.Point`. -/

/-- **The points of a complex elliptic curve form a real Lie group**: the complex Lie group
`W.Point`, written multiplicatively, with scalars restricted to `ℝ`. -/
instance (n : ℕ∞ω) : LieGroup 𝓘(ℝ, ℂ) n (Multiplicative W.Point) :=
  LieGroup.restrictScalars_self (𝕜 := ℂ) ℝ

section Exp

local instance : ConnectedSpace (Multiplicative W.Point) :=
  inferInstanceAs (ConnectedSpace W.Point)

/-- The real left-invariant derivations of `E(ℂ)` are identified with its tangent space `ℂ` at the
origin. -/
private noncomputable def derivationEquiv :
    ℂ ≃L[ℝ] LeftInvariantDerivation 𝓘(ℝ, ℂ) (Multiplicative W.Point) :=
  (leftInvariantDerivationEquivGroupLieAlgebra (I := 𝓘(ℝ, ℂ)) (G := Multiplicative W.Point)
    BoundarylessManifold.isInteriorPoint).symm.toContinuousLinearEquiv

/-! ### The exponential map -/

/-- **The exponential map `exp_E : ℂ → E(ℂ)` of a complex elliptic curve.** It sends a tangent
vector `v ∈ ℂ` at the origin to the time-one value, starting at the origin, of the flow of the
invariant vector field generated by `v` (`WeierstrassCurve.Projective.exp_eq_mulInvariantExp`). It
is the exponential of `E(ℂ)` regarded as a commutative real Lie group, hence a homomorphism. -/
noncomputable def exp : ℂ →+ W.Point :=
  ((AddEquiv.additiveMultiplicative (G := W.Point)).toAddMonoidHom.comp
    (TauCeti.lieExpAddMonoidHom (I := 𝓘(ℝ, ℂ)) (G := Multiplicative W.Point))).comp
    (derivationEquiv W).toLinearEquiv.toAddMonoidHom

/-- `exp_E` is the tangent-space exponential of `E(ℂ)` regarded as a real Lie group: the time-one
value of the invariant integral curve through the origin generated by a tangent vector. -/
theorem exp_eq_mulInvariantExp (z : ℂ) :
    W.exp z = (mulInvariantExp (I := 𝓘(ℝ, ℂ)) (G := Multiplicative W.Point) z).toAdd := by
  rw [exp, AddMonoidHom.comp_apply, AddMonoidHom.comp_apply, TauCeti.lieExpAddMonoidHom_apply,
    lieExp_eq_mulInvariantExp]
  exact congrArg (fun v ↦ Multiplicative.toAdd
    (mulInvariantExp (I := 𝓘(ℝ, ℂ)) (G := Multiplicative W.Point) v))
    (LinearEquiv.apply_symm_apply _ z)

private theorem coe_exp : ⇑W.exp =
    Multiplicative.toAdd ∘ lieExp (I := 𝓘(ℝ, ℂ)) (G := Multiplicative W.Point) ∘
      derivationEquiv W := by
  ext z
  simp only [exp, AddMonoidHom.coe_comp, Function.comp_apply, TauCeti.lieExpAddMonoidHom_apply,
    AddEquiv.coe_toAddMonoidHom, AddEquiv.additiveMultiplicative_apply, toMul_ofMul]
  -- What remains is that `(derivationEquiv W).toLinearEquiv.toAddMonoidHom` and
  -- `derivationEquiv W` have the same underlying function.
  rfl

/-- **`exp_E` is the sum of two invariant integral curves of `E(ℂ)`**, those through the origin
generated by the tangent vectors `1` and `i`: `exp_E (x + i y)` is the sum in `E(ℂ)` of the value at
time `x` of the first and the value at time `y` of the second. -/
theorem exp_eq_mulInvariantIntegralCurve_re_add_im (z : ℂ) :
    W.exp z =
      (mulInvariantIntegralCurve (I := 𝓘(ℝ, ℂ)) (G := Multiplicative W.Point)
          (1 : ℂ) 1 z.re).toAdd +
        (mulInvariantIntegralCurve (I := 𝓘(ℝ, ℂ)) (G := Multiplicative W.Point)
          I 1 z.im).toAdd := by
  have hz : z = z.re • (1 : ℂ) + z.im • I := by simp [Complex.re_add_im]
  conv_lhs => rw [hz]
  rw [map_add, exp_eq_mulInvariantExp, exp_eq_mulInvariantExp]
  exact congrArg₂ (fun a b ↦ Multiplicative.toAdd a + Multiplicative.toAdd b)
    (mulInvariantExp_smul (I := 𝓘(ℝ, ℂ)) (G := Multiplicative W.Point) (1 : ℂ) z.re)
    (mulInvariantExp_smul (I := 𝓘(ℝ, ℂ)) (G := Multiplicative W.Point) I z.im)

/-- **`exp_E` is surjective**, as `E(ℂ)` is connected. -/
theorem exp_surjective : Function.Surjective W.exp := by
  rw [coe_exp]
  exact Multiplicative.toAdd.surjective.comp
    ((TauCeti.lieExp_surjective_of_preconnectedSpace (I := 𝓘(ℝ, ℂ))
      (G := Multiplicative W.Point)).comp (derivationEquiv W).surjective)

/-- `exp_E` is continuous. -/
@[fun_prop]
theorem continuous_exp : Continuous W.exp := by
  rw [coe_exp]
  exact continuous_toAdd.comp
    ((contMDiff_lieExp (I := 𝓘(ℝ, ℂ)) (G := Multiplicative W.Point)).continuous.comp
      (derivationEquiv W).continuous)

/-- `exp_E` is an open map. -/
theorem isOpenMap_exp : IsOpenMap W.exp := by
  rw [coe_exp]
  exact isOpenMap_toAdd.comp
    ((TauCeti.isOpenMap_lieExp (I := 𝓘(ℝ, ℂ)) (G := Multiplicative W.Point)).comp
      (derivationEquiv W).toHomeomorph.isOpenMap)

/-! ### The kernel of the exponential map -/

private theorem coe_ker_exp : (W.exp.ker : Set ℂ) =
    derivationEquiv W ⁻¹'
      (TauCeti.lieExpAddMonoidHom (I := 𝓘(ℝ, ℂ)) (G := Multiplicative W.Point)).ker := by
  ext z
  simp [coe_exp]

/-- The kernel of `exp_E` is the pullback of the kernel of the Lie exponential. -/
private theorem toIntSubmodule_ker_exp : W.exp.ker.toIntSubmodule =
    ZLattice.comap ℝ
      (TauCeti.lieExpAddMonoidHom (I := 𝓘(ℝ, ℂ)) (G := Multiplicative W.Point)).ker.toIntSubmodule
      (derivationEquiv W).toLinearMap := by
  rw [← SetLike.coe_set_eq, ZLattice.coe_comap, AddSubgroup.coe_toIntSubmodule,
    AddSubgroup.coe_toIntSubmodule, coe_ker_exp]
  rfl

/-- **The kernel of `exp_E` is discrete.** -/
instance : DiscreteTopology W.exp.ker := by
  rw [← W.exp.ker.toIntSubmodule_toAddSubgroup, toIntSubmodule_ker_exp]
  infer_instance

/-- **The kernel of `exp_E` spans `ℂ` over `ℝ`**, as `E(ℂ)` is compact. -/
theorem span_ker_exp_eq_top : Submodule.span ℝ (W.exp.ker : Set ℂ) = ⊤ := by
  rw [← AddSubgroup.coe_toIntSubmodule, toIntSubmodule_ker_exp]
  exact IsZLattice.span_top

end Exp

/-- **The kernel of `exp_E` is a full lattice in `ℂ`.** -/
instance : IsZLattice ℝ W.exp.ker.toIntSubmodule where
  span_top := by
    rw [AddSubgroup.coe_toIntSubmodule]
    exact W.span_ker_exp_eq_top

/-- **The kernel of `exp_E` is the lattice of a period pair.** -/
theorem exists_periodPair_lattice_eq_ker_exp :
    ∃ L : PeriodPair, L.lattice = W.exp.ker.toIntSubmodule :=
  PeriodPair.exists_lattice_eq_of_isZLattice _

/-- **`exp_E` is a covering map.** It is an open continuous surjective homomorphism with discrete
kernel. -/
theorem isCoveringMap_exp : IsCoveringMap W.exp :=
  ((W.isOpenMap_exp.isQuotientMap W.continuous_exp
    W.exp_surjective).isAddQuotientCoveringMap_of_isDiscrete_ker_addMonoidHom
    (SetLike.isDiscrete_iff_discreteTopology.mpr inferInstance)).isCoveringMap

/-- **`E(ℂ)` is the quotient of `ℂ` by the kernel of `exp_E`**, as topological groups. -/
noncomputable def expQuotientKerContinuousAddEquiv : ℂ ⧸ W.exp.ker ≃ₜ+ W.Point :=
  ContinuousAddEquiv.quotientKerEquivOfSurjective W.exp
    (W.isOpenMap_exp.isStrictMap W.continuous_exp) W.exp_surjective

@[simp]
theorem expQuotientKerContinuousAddEquiv_mk (z : ℂ) :
    W.expQuotientKerContinuousAddEquiv (QuotientAddGroup.mk z) = W.exp z := by
  rw [expQuotientKerContinuousAddEquiv, ContinuousAddEquiv.quotientKerEquivOfSurjective_apply_mk]

@[simp]
theorem expQuotientKerContinuousAddEquiv_symm_exp (z : ℂ) :
    W.expQuotientKerContinuousAddEquiv.symm (W.exp z) = QuotientAddGroup.mk z := by
  rw [ContinuousAddEquiv.symm_apply_eq, expQuotientKerContinuousAddEquiv_mk]

/-! ### Holomorphy of the exponential map -/

/-- **The derivative of `exp_E` at `0` is the identity**: read in the chart of `E(ℂ)` at the
origin, `exp_E` has complex derivative the identity at `0`. -/
theorem hasFDerivAt_extChartAt_exp_zero :
    HasFDerivAt (fun z ↦ extChartAt 𝓘(ℂ) (0 : W.Point) (W.exp z)) (ContinuousLinearMap.id ℂ ℂ)
      0 := by
  simp_rw [exp_eq_mulInvariantExp]
  -- The type tag `Multiplicative W.Point` carries the charts of `W.Point`
  -- (`Multiplicative.chartedSpace`), so this is the chart at the identity of the type tag.
  exact hasFDerivAt_extChartAt_mulInvariantExp_zero_complex (E := ℂ)
    (G := Multiplicative W.Point)

/-- **`exp_E` is an entire map** `ℂ → E(ℂ)`: it is `C^n` over `ℂ` for every `n`, including
`n = ω`. -/
theorem contMDiff_exp (n : ℕ∞ω) : ContMDiff 𝓘(ℂ) 𝓘(ℂ) n W.exp := by
  rw [funext W.exp_eq_mulInvariantExp]
  -- The type tag `Multiplicative W.Point` carries the charts of `W.Point`
  -- (`Multiplicative.chartedSpace`), and `Multiplicative.toAdd` is the identity on points.
  exact TauCeti.contMDiff_mulInvariantExp_complex (E := ℂ) (G := Multiplicative W.Point) n

/-- **`exp_E` is holomorphic.** -/
theorem mdifferentiable_exp : MDifferentiable 𝓘(ℂ) 𝓘(ℂ) W.exp :=
  (W.contMDiff_exp 1).mdifferentiable one_ne_zero

end WeierstrassCurve.Projective
